with Columbian culture.

00:02:46.865 --> 00:02:48.810
- That's so cute.
- And yeah, I'm just like.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.820
But they love it and it's a cool way

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.880
to introduce it to
people that aren't aware.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.790
And it's also a cool way,
like representation, like-

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.900
- Yeah.
- Did you have anything

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.460
like that when you were
a kid to look up to?

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.980
Anybody that, you know?
- Yeah, I mean, I think

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.010
there's very few people that speak to you.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.810
And specifically for
the Latinos, you know,

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.570
and back in film and television,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:11.490
most of the characters that were portrayed

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.250
were unfortunately, not necessarily

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.052
who you wanted to grow up being, you know?

00:03:16.052 --> 00:03:16.885
- Yeah, they were like typecast.

00:03:16.885 --> 00:03:18.750
- Yeah, they were very typecast.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.780
They were very stereotypical
characters, you know?

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.690
But when I look back, I look
at this one man, you know,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.048
Desi Arnaz, right, who played

00:03:26.048 --> 00:03:27.353
Ricky Ricardo, yeah.
- Oh my god, I was obsessed

00:03:27.353 --> 00:03:28.650
with "I Love Lucy".

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.840
- Yeah, and I was obsessed too.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.000
And you know, I used
to watch it in Spanish,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.183
which was-
- I did not.

00:03:34.183 --> 00:03:36.060
- A really, really funny.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:37.740
I used to watch it in
Spanish back in Venezuela.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.590
And I'll tell you something
that's really, really unique.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.120
When I looked at him and
I saw him on this show,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.570
he was speaking Spanish to
me as a child, you know?

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.910
And not until when I came
to the United States,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.930
I realized, "Oh, they're
speaking English", you know.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.140
But the point is that I
felt like I could be him.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.030
- Yeah.
- And that I felt like

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.930
that was someone that sounded
like me, that looked like me.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.640
And originally, you know,
he was speaking in Spanish,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.920
so I was like, "Oh, anybody
can be that now, you know?"

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.300
And then you come to the United States

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.270
and you watch him do his
thing with an accent.

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.450
And so he was someone that inspired me,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:17.880
not only as an artist, but
also as an entrepreneur

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.260
and as a producer and-
- Yeah.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.050
- He's someone that done
so much for television.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:22.883
I don't know if you guys knew,

00:04:22.883 --> 00:04:25.410
but he created the multi-camera,

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.870
multi-cam format for sitcoms.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.370
- You can watch the
documentary and find that out,

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:31.050
and the movie, there's
like two different things.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:32.370
The documentary is just as cool as well

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.203
about the whole
- It's crazy cool, yeah.

00:04:33.203 --> 00:04:35.880
- Yeah, I got my mother and
I just deep dove on Christmas

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.713
and just watched the whole thing.

00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:37.860
We were like, "Oh my God!"

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.750
Like, 'cause I loved this show growing up.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.370
Like most kids would wanna watch

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:42.690
something you're not supposed to watch.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.400
I would go past my bedtime

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.550
'cause it'd come on at 10:30
p.m. to watch "I Love Lucy".

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.143
Like I, that's who I am.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:51.810
- But what a magic combination.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:56.400
When you think about
Lucy and Desi together.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.636
Well, Ricky and Lucy, right?

00:04:57.636 --> 00:05:00.420
- The chemistry, yeah.
- This chemistry, this

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:01.960
which was beyond culture back then.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.240
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- It was beyond what

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.073
you looked like.

00:05:04.073 --> 00:05:05.670
It was just an energy.
- [Kelly] It was so different.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.590
- Yeah.
- [Kelly] Yeah, I loved it.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.156
So you guys have shot like
450 episodes of "NCIS".

00:05:11.156 --> 00:05:12.690
Like what's it like to be a part

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.670
of such a magical crew and magical show?

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.010
- First of all, you know,
this is big shout out

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.620
and big hello to the crew

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.833
and every actor that share
the screen on that show.

00:05:22.833 --> 00:05:25.140
I've only been on the show seven years.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.540
The show's been on
- Only? (laughs)

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.950
- Well, I think I feel
like I'm always gonna

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:30.060
be the new guy because-

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.530
- It's been on for a long time, yeah.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.650
- This is their, they're
going into their 21st season.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.483
- [Kelly] Yeah.
(audience clapping)

00:05:35.483 --> 00:05:37.710
I love it.
- So they're doing 21 years,

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.750
right, it's like a long time.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.930
But it has been a great joy in my career

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.360
to experience that, to be
able to be in people's homes

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.370
with a show that's so beloved

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.740
and a show that knows itself so well

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.200
and understands that it's not
about the case of the week,

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.663
it's about your favorite
people solving it.

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.660
And I think that's the secret sauce.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.550
And you know, the fact that we're still

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.010
averaging 11 million a
week is unbelievable.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:03.390
- [Kelly] That's incredible.
- Yeah.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:07.770
- Yeah. So you also do
something called My Hour a Day.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:09.800
What's that? I want this.
- Yeah.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.160
Yeah.
- What is this?

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.000
It feels like a good kind of selfish.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.945
- I deserve this. Yes, I deserve this.

00:06:13.945 --> 00:06:15.290
- Yeah.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:20.290
- So, you know, and I know
you can relate to this, Kelly.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.850
There was a time, excuse me,
there was a time in my life

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:30.120
where I just said, "What else can I do?

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.430
And how much more can I do?"

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:34.500
And I was traveling and shooting

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:36.900
multiple shows at the same time.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.480
I remember at the time I was
shooting "Minority Report"

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.580
and "From Dusk Till Dawn"
and "Grey's Anatomy"

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.050
all in the same week.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.650
And I was, I couldn't
even get on the freeway.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:48.630
I had to helicopter from set to set

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:50.793
just to make these jobs possible.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.990
And then, you know, and then
I had a production company

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:56.430
and I'm producing all
these things and whatever.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.280
And I was just going from
Monday through Monday, you know,

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.460
and I realized I started
getting, you know,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.900
whenever I had three days off,

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.230
that's when my body's like,
"You're out for three days".

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:08.160
You know?

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.360
- It like shuts down for you.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.230
- [Wilmer] It shuts down for you.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.940
- It's like, "Nope".
- [Wilmer] Yeah, that's it.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:12.773
- Yeah.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.920
- And I realized that there
was common denominator in there

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.150
that I wasn't getting enough rest,

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:21.330
that I wasn't allowing my
brain to reboot, to restart.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:23.280
And I started thinking about my day.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.010
I'm like, "Well, I'm running
out of hours in the day."

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.240
But that wasn't the excuse,

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.460
the excuse was that like I was, you know,

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.730
I wasn't dedicating some
time of the day to myself.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:37.290
So I thought. "If I don't
have one hour in 24 hours

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.900
to dedicate to myself".

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.180
It could be reading a book,
it could be going for a walk,

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.883
it could be just sitting,
it could be a nap, you know?

00:07:45.755 --> 00:07:46.890
Or it could be working out.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.863
You know, once I implemented that,

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:54.190
I realize how much your
body really appreciates it

00:07:55.272 --> 00:07:58.920
and even more critically, how
much your mind deserves it.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.800
- Yeah.
- And so I created this thing,

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.460
this hashtag called My
Hour a Day and you know,

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.470
I tag it on to all my
workouts and all that.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.270
And I've seen fans from around the world

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:11.720
jumping in and doing their
hashtag My Hour a Day.

00:08:13.133 --> 00:08:15.030
And it's created this
smaller community of people

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:18.753
that have finally said, "Okay,
you know, what's the hurry?

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.400
You know, and can I sit
with myself for an hour

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.790
and really think about, you
know, what I really want?"

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.000
You know, and then distribute
the rest of your day

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.190
based on the things that are
the most important to you.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.440
But the clarity only happens in that hour.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.930
So in that hour, start
doing an inventory of my day

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:39.577
and thinking like, "Oh, do
I really have to do this?"

00:08:39.577 --> 00:08:42.330
"Is that a job that I really should take?"

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:44.310
And if you don't have time
to make those decisions,

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:48.178
you will find yourself
working all the way through.

00:08:48.178 --> 00:08:49.680
(Kelly laughing)

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:51.510
- Like the people upstairs that know

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.633
what my weekend was are
dying laughing right now.

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:58.380
- You're going through it? Yeah.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:08:59.700
- I literally, this weekend I was like,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:01.027
I took time off and like was like,

00:09:01.027 --> 00:09:02.190
"Oh, I have too much going on."

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:04.200
And then I got right back in.
- [Wilmer] Yeah.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:07.560
- And it's, yeah. And it's just not good.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.060
It's like, 'cause you,

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.120
I think too, for creative people

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:13.770
and you said you have
production company, I do too.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:14.880
You love to be involved.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.377
You love to create, right?
- Yeah.

00:09:16.377 --> 00:09:19.650
- But it's like you just
stretch yourself so thin

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:21.900
and we don't get it, there's like less,

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.940
I was saying this earlier,
literally in the glam room,

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:26.790
I was like, "There's less
days ahead of a lot for us

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:28.350
than behind, you know?"
- Right, right.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.790
- So it's like, and we just don't know.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:33.127
So it's like, how are we
choosing to, like use that time?

00:09:33.127 --> 00:09:34.740
- [Wilmer] Yeah.
- And like having kids,

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:35.573
like, it's like you wanna

00:09:35.573 --> 00:09:38.310
- And then you add you have to enjoy that.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:39.180
- [Kelly] And you want to.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:40.410
- You gotta enjoy being a parent.

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:41.697
You gotta enjoy being at home
because that's why you're

00:09:41.697 --> 00:09:42.757
- And you don't enjoy going

00:09:42.757 --> 00:09:44.550
"I know I'm not supposed to be
working on the weekend, honey

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.494
but they're calling me."
- Yeah.

00:09:46.494 --> 00:09:47.327
- It's like, yeah.
- [Wilmer] Yeah.

00:09:47.327 --> 00:09:48.720
- So it's a hard thing to do.

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:49.740
To implement.
- It's a hard thing to do.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.290
And I'll tell you this, and
then for anybody at home who's,

